/* ==============================================================
   02 — StartProgram: structure without flow control
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     Meca500 only (R3 or R4)
   TEACHES   StartProgram, program naming, and how to architect
             a cell around a robot that has no IF statement
   NEEDS     Robot activated + homed; the sub-programs below must
             be saved on the robot first (see SETUP)
   WATCH     Mostly a reading exercise. Read the comments.
   TIME      ~20 s once set up
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE ONE STRUCTURING TOOL

       StartProgram("name")
       StartProgram("folder/subfolder/name")

   It runs another program that is saved on the robot. That is
   the entire mechanism for reuse — there is no subroutine, no
   parameter passing, and no return value.

   Which sounds thin until you see what it is for.

   FOLDERS

   Programs are saved on the robot by name, and a slash in the
   name creates a folder. So a cell can be organised:

       demo/pick
       demo/place
       demo/home
       station_a/load
       station_b/load

   Names are case sensitive, up to 63 characters, from the
   alphanumeric characters plus underscore. Stick to letters,
   digits and underscores and you will never have to think about
   it. The .mxprog extension belongs to the file on your PC — the
   program name on the robot has no extension.

   THE ARCHITECTURE THIS IMPLIES

   Because there is no branching, the shape of a well-built
   Mecademic cell is always the same:

       HOST (PC / IPC / PLC)          ROBOT
       -------------------------      ----------------------
       reads sensors                  holds motion sequences
       runs the state machine   --->  executes them on request
       decides what happens next      reports checkpoints
       handles errors and recovery
       logs, HMI, recipes

   The host holds every decision. The robot holds every
   trajectory. StartProgram is the interface between them, and a
   checkpoint is how the robot answers.

   A cycle looks like:

       host: StartProgram("demo/pick")
       host: wait for checkpoint [3030][10]
       host: read the vision result, decide where to place
       host: SetVariable(demo.place, <the pose>)
       host: StartProgram("demo/place")
       host: wait for checkpoint [3030][20]

   All the logic is in the host. All the motion is in named,
   version-controlled, individually testable robot programs.

   WHY THIS IS A GOOD ANSWER, NOT AN EXCUSE

   Reps get asked "why can't the robot do IF?" — here is the
   honest answer to give:

     - The customer's logic already lives in their PLC or PC.
       Splitting a state machine across two devices is how
       cells become unmaintainable.
     - The host language is one they already know, with an IDE,
       a debugger, unit tests and version control. A proprietary
       robot language has none of that.
     - Motion sequences stay small, named and testable. You can
       run demo/pick on its own to check it.
     - It scales: the same host code drives four robots.

   The pitch is that the Meca500 is a precision motion component
   with an excellent API, not a controller pretending to be a
   PLC. Customers who want a teach pendant and a proprietary
   language are not the target, and that is fine.

   ------------------------------------------------------------
   SETUP FOR THIS FILE

   StartProgram only finds programs already saved ON THE ROBOT.
   Before running this, create and save three small programs
   using the MecaPortal program manager:

     demo/home
         SetJointVel(20)
         MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
         SetCheckpoint(1)

     demo/pick
         SetCartLinVel(60)
         MovePose(190, 0, 290, 0, 90, 0)
         MoveLin(190, 0, 250, 0, 90, 0)
         Delay(0.5)
         MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -40, 0, 0, 0)
         SetCheckpoint(10)

     demo/place
         SetCartLinVel(60)
         MovePose(190, 60, 290, 0, 90, 0)
         MoveLin(190, 60, 250, 0, 90, 0)
         Delay(0.5)
         MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -40, 0, 0, 0)
         SetCheckpoint(20)

   If they are not saved, StartProgram reports [3017], no offline
   program saved.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- A cycle, assembled from named blocks ----------------------
   This is what the host would issue, one call at a time, waiting
   on the checkpoint after each. Here they run back to back so
   you can watch the whole cycle. */

/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode — so this
   program starts from firmware defaults no matter what ran
   before it. Reactivating brings the motors back.

   This does NOT move the arm: a robot that was already homed
   needs no re-homing. (With an MEGP 25* gripper fitted, the
   gripper re-homes on reactivation. The robot still does not
   move — but keep the fingers clear.)

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision and calibration config all
   survive. Run common/cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
Home()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


StartProgram("demo/home")

StartProgram("demo/pick")

StartProgram("demo/place")

StartProgram("demo/home")


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Open demo/pick in the program manager and run it on its
      own. Being able to test one motion block in isolation,
      without running the cell, is a genuine engineering
      advantage — say so.

   2. Edit demo/pick, save, and run this file again. The cycle
      picks up the change with nothing else touched.

   3. When the "no flow control" question comes — and it always
      comes — do not get defensive. Draw the two-column diagram
      from the comments above. Most integrators nod, because it
      is how they already build machines.

   NEXT   03_pick_and_place.mxprog — everything so far, in one
          program.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
